snippet.description:
Swell after swell is hitting the West Coast. On Saturday, November 14, 2026, waves topped the seawall at Rockaway Beach in Pacifica and pushed water hundreds of feet inland, nearly a foot of water covered Highway 1, and a wave shredded a railing on the Capitola Wharf, rebuilt for more than $8 million after it split in two in January 2023. The Point Reyes buoy measured 31.2 feet, its highest reading since 2015, the National Weather Service warned of breakers up to 35 feet with occasional 40 to 60 foot waves from San Francisco to Big Sur, and Oregon's central coast was under a warning for 30 to 35 foot breakers.

NOAA's Climate Prediction Center says El Niño is here, with Niño 3.4 about 2.5°C above normal and about a 60 percent chance of a historic event. In this video we go swell by swell: where the water came over the walls, how a storm far out in the North Pacific floods a street in California, why El Niño puts the coast in the bullseye, the midweek storm with 40 to 50 foot waves possible, and the Thanksgiving week king tides, all with video filmed by people on the coast.

Daily Weather Report covers natural disasters and extreme weather across the United States as they develop, using sourced reporting and avoiding unsupported speculation.

CHAPTERS
00:00 — Pacifica: The Ocean in the Streets
03:41 — Capitola: The Rebuilt Wharf Gets Hit
06:04 — How a Storm Far Away Floods a Street
07:47 — 31.2 Feet: What the Buoys Measured
08:41 — Mavericks to Southern California
10:54 — Oregon: 30-35 Foot Breakers
13:08 — El Niño: Why California Is in the Bullseye
15:02 — High Tide + El Niño + Swell = Flooding
16:08 — Swell by Swell: What Comes Next
17:25 — The Midweek Storm: 40-50 Foot Waves Possible
18:49 — Thanksgiving King Tides
20:16 — Stay Safe

SOURCES
